CompTIA Security 2.4 

2.3 (vulnerabilities). 2.4 is about the attacks that use them, and the signs that tell you an attack is happening.
This is the most SOC-analyst part of the whole exam, because spotting those signs is what a SOC analyst does all day.

Analogy: if 2.3 was knowing a patient’s risk factors, 2.4 is reading the symptoms and working out what’s actually wrong.

It’s a big objective with a lot of videos, but they fall into a few groups:

Malware: viruses, worms, ransomware, trojans, spyware, bloatware, keyloggers, logic bombs and rootkits. Each type behaves differently.
Physical attacks: brute force (literally breaking in), RFID cloning (copying building access cards), and environmental attacks (like targeting power or cooling).
Network attacks: denial of service, DNS attacks, wireless attacks, on-path (man-in-the-middle) attacks, replay attacks and credential replay.
Application attacks: injection, buffer overflows, privilege escalation, request forgery and directory traversal. You’ll recognise some of these from 2.3. This time the focus is on what the attack looks like in action.
Cryptographic and password attacks: downgrade attacks, collisions, birthday attacks, password spraying and brute force.
Indicators of compromise (IOCs): the warning signs, such as account lockouts, impossible travel (logging in from Ballarat and then Brazil ten minutes later), missing logs, resource spikes and blocked content.
